-- Lesson 1: Translating Words to Math
'{"hook":"Every math problem starts as a real-world question in words. The ancient art of translating language into equations — algebra — was formalized by a Persian scholar whose name literally gave us the word ''algorithm.''","inventor":{"name":"Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi","birth_year":780,"death_year":850,"nationality":"Persian","bio":"A scholar at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad who wrote al-Kitab al-Mukhtasar fi Hisab al-Jabr wal-Muqabala (The Compendious Book on Calculation by Completion and Balancing) around 820 CE. This book gave us the word ''algebra'' and systematized solving word problems with equations."},"year_invented":820,"etymology":{"word":"algebra","from":"Arabic","parts":[{"root":"al-jabr","meaning":"restoration, completion (moving terms to the other side)"},{"root":"al-muqabala","meaning":"balancing (simplifying equal terms on both sides)"}]},"story_paragraphs":["In 820 CE Baghdad, the caliph al-Ma''mun asked al-Khwarizmi to write a practical math book. The problems were real: inheritance disputes, land measurements, trade calculations. All started as word problems.","Al-Khwarizmi''s genius was creating a systematic method to convert any word problem into an equation, then solve it step by step. He called the method al-jabr (restoration) — moving terms across the equals sign — which became our word ''algebra.''","His name, al-Khwarizmi, was later Latinized to ''Algoritmi'' — which became our word ''algorithm,'' meaning a step-by-step procedure. Every Google search, every GPS route, every recommendation engine runs on algorithms — named after this one scholar.","Today, translating words to math is still the most important skill in applied mathematics. Engineers, doctors, economists, and scientists all start with a real-world question and convert it to equations they can solve."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Diophantus","contribution":"Greek mathematician (~250 CE) who solved word problems using symbolic notation, earning the title ''father of algebra''"},{"name":"Fibonacci","contribution":"Brought al-Khwarizmi''s methods to Europe through his 1202 book Liber Abaci"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Shopping","icon":"🛒","description":"''If 3 shirts cost $45, how much is each?'' → 3x = 45 → x = 15"},{"name":"Cooking","icon":"🍳","description":"''Double a recipe for 4 to serve 8'' → multiply all quantities by 2"},{"name":"Travel","icon":"✈️","description":"''How long to drive 300 km at 60 km/h?'' → time = 300 ÷ 60 = 5 hours"},{"name":"Finance","icon":"💰","description":"''Save $50/week for a $600 laptop'' → 50w = 600 → w = 12 weeks"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Page from al-Khwarizmi''s Algebra manuscript","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Image-Al-Kit%C4%81b_al-mukhta%E1%B9%A3ar_f%C4%AB_%E1%B8%A5is%C4%81b_al-%C4%9Fabr_wa-l-muq%C4%81bala.jpg"}}'::jsonb)
